  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Sierra Nevada has $1.9B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The company won 40 awards so far this fiscal year, with an average award size of $48M. Obligations are down 14.5% year over year. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, accounting for $1.9B of obligations. Aircraft Manufacturing is the largest market by dollars, with $1.5B obligated to that NAICS. Other notable markets include All Other Professional, Scientific, and Technical Services at $333M and Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing at $115M. FY trends show growth into FY25 followed by a partial FY26 total: FY21 obligations were $112M, FY22 obligations were $539M, FY23 obligations were $699M, FY24 obligations were $1.3B, FY25 obligations were $2.2B, and FY26 obligations are $1.9B. Smaller market areas include Computer Systems Design Services at $5.4M and All Other Telecommunications at $3.8M. This snapshot covers obligations only, with award-value figures in some tables noted as total contract values.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies buy from Sierra Nevada?",
        "body": "Department of Defense is the largest buyer, with $1.9B in obligations. Department of Homeland Security is a distant second, with $35K in obligations. Agency buying is concentrated, so most federal spending for Sierra Nevada is DoD-driven."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What product and service markets does Sierra Nevada serve?",
        "body": "Aircraft Manufacturing is the primary market, with $1.5B obligated. Sierra Nevada also serves professional and technical services at $333M, and search and detection systems at $115M, plus smaller amounts in computer systems design, telecommunications, and R&D."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Sierra Nevada make from federal contracts?",
      "a": "Sierra Nevada has $1.9B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Sierra Nevada won 40 awards this fiscal year, with an average award size of $48M, and obligations are down 14.5% year over year. Historical obligations were $112M in FY21, $539M in FY22, $699M in FY23, $1.3B in FY24, and $2.2B in FY25."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which agencies buy from Sierra Nevada?",
      "a": "The Department of Defense buys most from Sierra Nevada, with $1.9B in obligations. The Department of Homeland Security also buys from Sierra Nevada, with $35K in obligations."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Sierra Nevada sell to the government?",
      "a": "Sierra Nevada sells aircraft-related products and services and related systems. Aircraft Manufacturing accounts for $1.5B in obligations, All Other Professional, Scientific, and Technical Services accounts for $333M, and Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing accounts for $115M, with smaller obligations in computer systems design, telecommunications, and R&D."
